  "texto": "# The descent\n@energy 2\n> Narrate calmly, like opening a book at night.\n\nMarina had been piloting the small research submarine for twelve years.\nThat morning, the sea was still and the sky was a pearly gray.\nFrom the ship, Julio checked the oxygen, the lights and the radio one last time.\n\"This is your last dive before you retire,\" he said with a smile.\n\"Then let's make it **unforgettable**,\" she answered.\nThe hatch closed with a heavy thud.\nThe submarine began to sink, and the blue grew darker and darker.\nAt two hundred meters, the sunlight had almost disappeared.\nAt a thousand, there was only **blackness** and the hum of the engines.\nMarina switched on the floodlights and watched white specks drift by, like snow.\n[soft]She loved that silence more than anything in the world.[/soft]\n\n# The blackout\n@energy 3\n> Don't dramatize yet: the tension builds slowly.\n\nAt two thousand meters, an alarm broke the calm.\nThe lights flickered and, all at once, **everything** went dark.\n\"Julio, I've lost main power,\" Marina said, without raising her voice.\nThe radio crackled for a few endless seconds.\n\"I hear you. How much oxygen do you have left?\" [up]\n\"About six hours,\" she replied.\nIt was enough to surface, but without the engines she couldn't move.\nThe submarine floated motionless, hanging in the dark like a stone in the air.\n[pause 2s]\nThen she saw something that **wasn't** in any manual.\nA blue light switched on in front of the window, then another, and another.\nThey were jellyfish, lanternfish and tiny squid, all glowing to the same **rhythm**.\n\n# The dance of lights\n@energy 4\n> The climax: more energy, but no shouting.\n\n\"Julio, you have to see this!\" she cried, forgetting her fear.\nThe lights spun around the submarine, forming a perfect **circle**.\nThen they all moved upward, as if they were showing her the way.\nMarina remembered a rule her mother taught her: when you don't know what to do, watch.\nShe released the emergency weights, and the submarine began to rise.\nThe lights went with her, climbing beside her meter by meter.\n\"You're doing great, keep going!\" Julio repeated from the surface, his voice breaking.\n[pause 2s]\nAt five hundred meters, the animals switched off **all at once**.\nMaybe they didn't need her anymore, or maybe she didn't need them.\nAbove her, the water turned gray, then green, then a **brilliant** blue.\n\n# Back on deck\n@energy 2\n> The calm returns. End slowly.\n\nWhen she opened the hatch, the cold air hit her face.\nJulio helped her out onto the platform and hugged her without a word.\nAfter a while, he asked in a low voice: \"What happened down there?\" [up]\nMarina looked at the sea for a long time.\n[soft]\"They brought me back,\" she whispered.[/soft]\nNo one on the ship fully understood that answer.\nThat night she wrote the dive report with exact data: depth, time and temperature.\nBut she kept one page **separate**, just for herself.\nOn that page, she drew a circle of blue lights, her private treasure.\nYears later, people at the harbor still tell the story of the pilot the deep sea guided home.\nAnd whenever she hears it, she smiles and doesn't **correct** a thing. [down]\n"
