  "texto": "# The garage\n@energy 2\n> Narrate tenderly, without hurry.\n\nGrandpa Ernesto kept a red bicycle in the garage, covered in dust.\nSofia found it one Saturday while looking for a box of Christmas decorations.\nIt had flat tires, a rusty chain and a broken seat.\nSofia asked out loud: \"Whose bicycle is this?\" [up]\nHer grandfather leaned in the doorway and smiled **slowly**.\n\"It was mine. I used to ride it to the sea when I was your age.\"\nHe told her how he crossed the village pedaling, with the wind in his face.\nThen came work, family and the years, and the bicycle was forgotten.\n\"It's beyond **repair**,\" he said with a shrug.\nSofia didn't answer.\nBut that night, in her notebook, she wrote a secret **plan**.\nIn one month, her grandfather would turn eighty.\n\n# The missing piece\n@energy 3\n> The tension grows: something could go wrong.\n\nEvery afternoon, Sofia went to see Raul, the neighborhood mechanic, at his workshop.\nTogether they sanded off the rust, painted the frame and changed the wheels.\n\"You've got good hands,\" he told her, watching her adjust the brakes.\nBut there was a problem: the left pedal was **cracked** in two.\nThe model was so old that nobody made spare parts anymore.\nThey searched in shops, at flea markets and online, with no luck.\n[pause 2s]\nThere were three days left until the birthday.\nSofia sat in the workshop doorway, ready to give up.\nJust at that moment, Raul appeared with a **wooden** box full of old parts.\n\"I've kept this for forty years,\" he said, blowing off the dust.\nInside, among screws and bolts, was an **identical** pedal.\n\n# The birthday\n@energy 4\n> The most moving moment: let your voice light up.\n\nOn the day of the party, the house was full of people, balloons and the smell of cake.\nHis granddaughter waited for everyone to finish singing, then asked for silence.\n\"Grandpa, close your eyes,\" she said, taking his hand.\nShe led him to the backyard, where the bicycle gleamed in the sun.\n[pause 2s]\nHe opened his eyes and said **nothing** for a long time.\nHe touched the handlebars, the new seat and the bell, as if they were made of glass.\n\"It's the same one!\" he exclaimed at last, his voice trembling.\n\"The same one I had when I was twelve!\"\nEveryone clapped as he climbed on, carefully, with Sofia's help.\nHe rode a **full** lap around the yard, laughing with pleasure like a child.\nNobody dared tell him to be careful.\n\n# The photo\n@energy 2\n> Intimate and warm. End with a smile.\n\nThat evening, he asked Sofia to sit beside him.\nHe lowered his voice and asked: \"Do you know why I stopped riding?\" [up]\nShe shook her head.\n[soft]\"Because I thought it was too late for the things that made me happy.\"[/soft]\nSofia hugged him tight.\nThat week they took a photo together by the sea, with the red bicycle between them.\nHe hung it on the living room wall.\nUnderneath, in his **big**, shaky handwriting, he wrote one sentence.\n\"It's never too late to start riding again.\"\nToday, the bicycle is still in the garage, but it no longer gathers dust.\nEvery Sunday, someone takes it for a ride down to the **beach**. [down]\n"
