  "texto": "# The trail\n@energy 2\n> A calm voice, like remembering a trip.\n\nAna and her brother Tomas arrived in Patagonia at the end of February.\nThey wanted to hike to a glacier that only came into view after six hours on the trail.\nThe park ranger watched them from the window of his wooden cabin.\n\"Check the forecast. Out here, the wind changes in minutes,\" he warned them.\nTomas laughed and tightened the straps of his backpack.\n\"We've got water, food and a map. What could go wrong?\" [up]\nThe morning was bright and the sky a **clean**, deep blue.\nThey walked among twisted trees, icy rivers and moss-covered stones.\nIn the distance, a condor drew wide circles above the hills.\nAna stopped every few **meters** to take photos, just for the pleasure of it.\nFor the first time in months, she felt she could breathe without hurry.\n\n# The storm\n@energy 3\n> The tension rises all at once.\n\nIn the middle of the afternoon, the clouds dropped down from the mountains.\nThe wind began to blow with a **force** Ana had never felt.\nThe gusts kept pushing them toward the edge of the trail.\n\"Get down!\" Tomas shouted, grabbing her arm.\nThey threw themselves to the ground behind a large rock, hearts racing.\nThe rain came in sideways, cold and hard, like tiny stones.\n[pause 2s]\nTomas tried to stand and fell with a **groan**.\nHe had twisted his ankle.\nAna looked at the soaked map, then at the sky, which was growing darker.\nThe nearest shelter was an hour away.\nWith his ankle like that, it would take at least **three**.\n\n# The shelter\n@energy 4\n> The climax: firmness and relief.\n\n\"Lean on me and don't look down,\" Ana said, with a calm she didn't feel.\nThey moved forward step by step, counting under their breath so they wouldn't think about the cold.\nEvery gust forced them to stop and wait.\nAna gathered what little energy she had left and kept going.\n[pause 2s]\nThrough the fog, all of a sudden, she saw a **yellow** light.\n\"There it is, Tomas, the shelter!\" she cried, almost out of voice.\nThe door opened before they reached it.\nIt was the park ranger, with a flashlight in one hand and a blanket over his shoulder.\n\"I've been looking for you for **two** hours,\" he said, helping them inside.\nIn the cabin, a wood stove crackled next to a pot of hot tea.\nAna dropped onto a bench and, at last, she **cried**.\n\n# The glacier\n@energy 2\n> Calm after the storm. End with a smile.\n\nThe next morning, the wind was gone as if it had never existed.\nThe ranger wrapped Tomas's ankle and served bowls of warm oatmeal.\n\"You don't conquer Patagonia. You respect it,\" he told them as he passed the bread.\nAna stepped out of the shelter with her mug in both hands.\n[soft]In front of her, the glacier shone with a blue that seemed to come from another world.[/soft]\nIt was the place they had come to find.\nIt was **right** there, a few kilometers away, but neither of them wanted to keep walking.\nTomas limped to the door and asked: \"Shall we come back next summer?\" [up]\nAna smiled and handed him the mug.\n\"We'll come back, but this time we'll check the **forecast**.\"\nThey both laughed, watching the ice in **silence**. [down]\n"
