  "texto": "# The shortcut\n@energy 2\n> Narrate with gentle humor.\n\nMateo trusted his phone more than any person.\nSo when his grandmother Elena suggested walking to the lake, he opened the map.\n\"It says forty minutes through the woods,\" he announced, very sure of himself.\nHis grandmother preferred the long way, the one she had known since she was a girl.\nMateo insisted: \"Why walk two hours if there's a shortcut?\" [up]\nElena agreed, though she slipped her father's old compass into her pocket.\nAt first, everything was perfect.\nThe **blue** dot moved across the screen, just as they moved among the trees.\nSounding like an expert, he explained that the phone listened to satellites flying very high.\n\"It works even without a **signal**,\" he said proudly.\nHis grandmother just raised an eyebrow.\n\n# The woods\n@energy 3\n> Worry grows.\n\nAn hour later, there was still no sign of the lake.\nThe trail had turned into a maze of fallen logs, mud and dry branches.\nMateo turned the phone from side to side, confused.\nThe blue dot jumped from place to place, as if it were **dizzy**.\n\"It's the trees; they block the signals,\" he muttered.\nThen the screen went black.\n[pause 2s]\nThe battery had hit **zero**.\nMateo felt a chill in his stomach.\nThe wind shook the treetops with a strange sound.\nIt was starting to get dark, and he had no idea where the house was.\nIn a small voice, he asked: \"So what do we do now?\" [up]\nHis grandmother took the compass out of her pocket without saying a **word**.\n\n# The compass\n@energy 4\n> The climax: relief and joy.\n\n\"It's a very old rule: water always runs down toward the lake,\" Elena explained.\n\"If we follow the **stream**, we'll get there.\"\nThey walked beside the water, listening to it rush over the stones.\nHis grandmother checked the compass every so often and corrected their course.\nHer grandson didn't say a word for a long while.\nOnly their footsteps, the water and a hidden bird broke the silence.\n[pause 2s]\nThen, between the trees, a **silver** glint appeared.\n\"The lake!\" Mateo shouted, running toward the shore.\nOn the other side were the dock and his grandmother's house, with the light on.\n\"Grandma, you're better than any app!\"\nElena laughed and ruffled his hair.\n\"Apps are useful, but you also have to look **around** you.\"\n\n# What he learned\n@energy 2\n> A quiet tone. End with affection.\n\nThat night, Mateo charged his phone and looked up why the blue dot had gone crazy.\nHe read that the system needs to see four satellites or more, whenever possible.\nAmong tall trees, buildings or mountains, the signals bounce and the calculation **fails**.\nThe next day, he downloaded a map of the woods and bought a spare battery.\nBut he did something more.\nHe asked his grandmother to teach him how to use the compass.\n[soft]She gave him her father's, a small treasure worn smooth by the years.[/soft]\n\"Now it's your turn to look after it,\" she told him.\nHe still uses the map on his phone, especially in the city.\nBut every time he goes into the woods, his grandmother's **compass** goes with him. [down]\n"
