  "texto": "# Before dawn\n@energy 2\n> A warm tone, with a little humor.\n\nJulia's alarm went off at four in the morning.\nIt was her first day at Ramon's bakery, the oldest one in the neighborhood.\nShe got dressed in the dark, half asleep and full of nerves.\nOn the way, she went over everything she had learned in class: flour, water, salt and patience.\nThe bakery smelled of firewood and fresh bread.\nRamon was waiting at the door, in a white apron and with a **serious** face.\n\"There's only one rule here: you don't have to be fast, you have to be **steady**,\" he said.\nHe showed her the ovens, the wooden tables and a notebook full of handwritten recipes.\n\"Today you'll make the bread rolls for the school party,\" he added.\nJulia swallowed hard.\nIt was **three hundred** rolls.\n\n# The mistake\n@energy 3\n> The tension rises.\n\nJulia kneaded dough for hours, her arms more tired by the minute.\nAt seven, she slid the first trays into the oven and sat down to rest for a minute.\nJust one minute.\n[pause 2s]\nWhen she opened her eyes, a burning smell filled the whole kitchen.\n\"No, no, no!\" she shouted, running to the oven.\nThe rolls were as **black** as coal.\nRamon walked in at that moment and looked at the trays without a word.\nJulia felt her face burning with shame.\n\"I'm so sorry. If you want, I'll leave right now,\" she said under her breath.\nThe baker crossed his arms and stared at her for a **long** time.\nThere were barely two hours left until the delivery.\n\n# Against the clock\n@energy 4\n> The climax: a quick pace and joy at the end.\n\n\"Leave? Don't even think about it,\" Ramon answered, rolling up his sleeves.\n\"You knead, I shape, and the oven is never left **alone**, not even for a second.\"\nThey worked without talking, at a pace Julia didn't think was possible.\nShe weighed the flour, and he shaped the rolls with quick, precise movements.\nEach tray that came out of the oven was more golden than the last.\n[pause 2s]\nAt nine o'clock sharp, the school van arrived.\nThree hundred rolls, warm and perfect, waited in baskets on the counter.\n\"We did it!\" Julia cried, with flour even on her eyebrows.\nThe school principal tasted one and closed her eyes with pleasure.\n\"This is the best bread in the **neighborhood**,\" she said.\nRamon winked at Julia and didn't correct **anyone**.\n\n# The notebook\n@energy 2\n> Intimate and funny. End slowly.\n\nAt the end of the day, Ramon poured her a coffee and opened his old notebook.\nOn the first page there was a **dark** stain and a date from forty years ago.\nHe asked her, with half a smile: \"Do you know what this is?\" [up]\nJulia shook her head.\n[soft]\"My first day. I burned all the bread in town.\"[/soft]\nShe laughed so hard she almost spilled her coffee.\n\"The mistake doesn't matter that much,\" he went on. \"What matters is what you do **next**.\"\nSince that day, Julia arrives every morning at four, without an alarm.\nShe has her own page in the notebook now, with her own recipe.\nAnd in one corner, she drew a small burnt roll.\nShe says it's so she never forgets her first **day**. [down]\n"
